yesterday we got news that the sec has  gotten an open letter from two  congressmen those two congressmen are  tom emmer who's a congressman from  minnesota and darren soto a congressman  from florida both of them wrote a letter  urging the sec to approve a spot etf if  you remember a couple of weeks ago we  actually got an approval of a bitcoin  etf the very first one but that is a  futures based etf it does not give you  exposure to the actual bitcoin itself  but more so the futures price we've seen  the performance of let's say the gbtc  product the bitcoin trust drastically  outperform bito which is the futures  based product one actually has exposure  to bitcoin the other only has exposure  to futures and there's all kinds of  differences this letter urges the sec  and says well if we have a bitcoin  futures etf then we should absolutely  have a bitcoin spot etf and so it's very  interesting to see these are two  congressmen these are not regulators  these are not people who are involved in  the financial markets these are  congressmen who are going ahead and  writing this letter now when you see  that there could just be political  stunts it could just be to get some  notoriety and some headlines but also i  think that there is an entire world  where people are starting to wake up to  the fact that their's constituents want  these assets they want exposure to it  and there may be financial firms or  institutions or maybe pensions  endowments or potentially even private  investors for one reason or another that  cannot get exposure to the actual  bitcoin themselves  and therefore they want bitcoin spot etf  exposure and so watching  congressmen start to send letters like  this is very interesting we have james  seaford who was on he was covering for  bloomberg all of the etf business that  said the next big etf decision to date  uh to watch is 11 14 which is two  sundays the sec will likely deny vanessa  application for a spot bitcoin etf but  the denial letter is likely to provide a  lot of valuable insights into how the  sec's current view of crypto and bitcoin  so when we see that we've got all of  these applications that keep coming in  over and over and over again of people  wanting to get that bitcoin spot etf  remember what we said a couple of weeks  ago having the futures based etf was a  big deal that meant that the sec and  other regulators had finally gotten  comfortable with there being an etf  structure to give people some exposure  now we're getting into the nitty-gritty  now we're getting into the details do it  should it be a spot exposure or a  futures exposure well obviously there  are people who want spot exposure so we  have two congressmen from two different  states both asking saying hey how can we  get this let's get this done do we think  that this is good  or it's completely agnostic and doesn't  matter if the congressman write the  letters or not like do we think this  actually has an impact on the decision  making given that there's a spot etf  application that is going to come up  here for decision in next couple of days  i think uh any noise is generally good  right positive noise trying to push it  forward but yeah i don't know how much  uh this will actually do i think is your  point real right like is the congress or  the congress uh men or women going to  make any difference in the approval  process no i don't think so but i think  that anytime you can see  demand from politicians demand from  business leaders demand from retail  traders etc it's a good thing and it  helps with that approval process showing  that people want this right and we've  already seen a future gtf approved spot  you would think is next we've had some  people on the show previously that think  that it's probably 12 to 24 maybe even  36 months away at the longest point um  so who knows when it'll actually come  through but there's obviously a high  demand for it and i think it would be  good for the market right we don't want  retail investors who want to own the  asset going out and buying the futures  etf that's just not that's not the best  vehicle to do that in right so you  either go buy regular bitcoin or give  them access to an etf uh another vehicle  where they can do this and not only  retailer investors right this is for  institutional traders give them access  to a vehicle where they can go do this  without owning bitcoin themselves in  their books so i think uh when it comes  down to is this going to help who knows  we're still probably a long ways away  from actually getting something improved  on the spot side but any kind of noise  that we can create to show demand i  think is a good thing  john tom and darren have been really pro  bitcoin for a while now i believe and  obviously this letter that they sent to  the sec is pro bitcoin um  and  they don't really have a lot of  influence on the sec right like they can  they can bang the drums and write these  letters and kind of put them publicly  but uh ultimately it's going to be the  sec's decision kind of when that gets  approved um and then one thing i want to  point out too is it wasn't like they  were saying a spot etf is better than a  futures etf actually a quote from their  thing is to be clear we do not intend to  say that one method of exposure is  better than the other so they're more  just saying that um  they described about the nuance  differences but they were also saying  that look if you prove to futures you  should also approve a spot etf as well  um to give people that exposure in the  market  i i do think that there's a very big  difference the thing that most people  don't understand here is one if we pull  up the letter one more time uh the  congressman explicitly state that they  are not claiming that uh one method of  exposure is better than the other right  what is what you're saying here the  thing that they are saying though uh  which i which i actually agree with is  that there are different there's  differences that investors should be  allowed to prove and i think that's your  point here john right is that like let  the investors choose which one is better  and so one of the big questions was  leading up to the etf approval was if  they only approved one etf applicant for  let's say three months then you're  basically king making that one etf  applicant as the winner now the question  goes which the sec did not do within the  first week they had multiple people in  the market but now the question is if  you are going ahead and saying hey the  future's not the spot right are you king  making in some way or you're not giving  investors choice now the art the counter  argument from the regulators which i  actually think there's a fair argument  from uh is we want to protect the  investors now are they actually  protecting investors are they not  they're experts on this stuff and so are  the etf applicants and so i think they  just need to get in a room and they need  to talk this out um in terms of what the  issues are and it comes down to this  like  uh um kind of the acts of which uh these  funds are constructed but ultimately i  think there will be a bitcoin spot etf i  think there will be a bitcoin futures  etf which we already have uh they'll  live in coexistence and it's actually  shocking because there wasn't that much  inflow to the bitcoin futures etf that  first two or three days there was tons  of flow like you know billion dollars or  so they haven't even hit two billion  dollars in the funds yet  right so like now we are a week or two  later and they kind of just plateau well  the hype has died so the hype is one  piece of it but it's not just hype right  part of it is like there was some  pent-up demand for a futures one but we  don't know if a spot had been approved  would there have been five billion  dollars that float we don't know and by  the way that might not be the best  metric to measure  is  how much capital flows the best me